\section{Introduction}

Developing assistive and fully autonomous vehicles requires robust real-time risk estimation. While statistical models and machine learning techniques like Random Forests and Support Vector Machines are employed for this purpose, they demand extensive labeled data and engineering effort. We propose employing Graphical Models, specifically Bayesian Networks, to perform risk estimation with minimal data. These models require only straightforward feature engineering, such as calculating the proximity of a vehicle to a crosswalk. Our method achieves state-of-the-art performance on the nuScenes [1] dataset and performs competitively on the Argoverse [2] dataset.

\section{Background}

\subsection{Risk Estimation}

Risk estimation involves predicting the likelihood of an event, such as an accident, and is essential for vehicle safety. Modern assistive and autonomous systems use these estimates to navigate more safely. Various approaches exist, including statistical methods [3], supervised learning [4], and hybrid solutions [5].

\subsection{Bayesian Networks}

A Bayesian Network (BN) [6] is a probabilistic graphical model that represents random variables as nodes and conditional dependencies as directed edges, encoding the joint probability distribution.

\subsection{Conditional Probability Tables}

To make BNs computationally feasible, clique potentials are incorporated. In this context, each feature is treated as a random variable, and a conditional probability table (CPT) is assigned to each.

